WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:03.202
-Our first guest tonight
is a Grammy-nominated musician

00:00:03.202 --> 00:00:04.837
and actor you know from movies
such as "Moonlight,"

00:00:04.837 --> 00:00:06.439
"Hidden Figures,"
and "Harriet."

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:08.941
She stars in "Glass Onion:
A Knives Out Mystery,"

00:00:08.941 --> 00:00:11.344
which begins streaming
on Netflix December 23rd.

00:00:11.344 --> 00:00:12.845
Let's take a look.

00:00:12.845 --> 00:00:16.115
-You're a detective, right?
-Yes. Um...

00:00:16.115 --> 00:00:18.585
-Can you spot the other thing,

00:00:18.585 --> 00:00:20.753
the real thing
this group has in common?

00:00:20.753 --> 00:00:22.355
-Randi, come on.

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.858
-Oh, Lionel. Everybody knows
who Lionel works for.

00:00:25.858 --> 00:00:28.060
That's no secret.

00:00:28.060 --> 00:00:30.830
And we know who bankrolled
Claire's campaign.

00:00:30.830 --> 00:00:33.532 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:33.532 --> 00:00:36.736
But when nobody --
nobody would touch Birdie

00:00:36.736 --> 00:00:40.339
with a 10-foot pole
because she went on "Oprah"

00:00:40.339 --> 00:00:42.609
and compared herself
to Harriet Tubman.

00:00:42.609 --> 00:00:44.444
-In spirit.

00:00:44.444 --> 00:00:48.081
-Please welcome back to the
show Janelle Monáe, everybody!

00:00:48.081 --> 00:00:49.816
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:49.816 --> 00:00:57.857 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:57.857 --> 00:01:05.865 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:05.865 --> 00:01:13.906 line:0%
♪♪

00:01:13.906 --> 00:01:18.411
-Resplendent as always, Janelle.
You look like a million dollars.

00:01:18.411 --> 00:01:20.413
-It's good to see you in person.
Can I touch you? Are you real?

00:01:20.413 --> 00:01:22.749
-It is. Last time we were here,
we Zoomed.

00:01:22.749 --> 00:01:24.483
-We Zoomed.
-And, by the way,

00:01:24.483 --> 00:01:26.885
you dressed up for Zoom as well,
which I really appreciated.

00:01:26.885 --> 00:01:28.354
-I did.
Because I like you a lot.

00:01:28.354 --> 00:01:30.756
-Well, thank you very much.
I like you a lot, as well.

00:01:30.756 --> 00:01:32.725
And seeing you here in person
is wonderful.

00:01:32.725 --> 00:01:35.028
And, also, I'm not the only one
who likes you a lot.

00:01:35.028 --> 00:01:38.931
Congratulations on your Critics'
Choice Award nomination today.

00:01:38.931 --> 00:01:40.166
[ Cheers and applause ]
-Oh. I know. Thank you.

00:01:40.166 --> 00:01:42.602
Thank you.
Thank you so much, man.

00:01:42.602 --> 00:01:44.403
-You both got
a personal nomination,

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:48.140
which must be so special,
but also an ensemble nomination.

00:01:48.140 --> 00:01:49.776
-Yes.
-And Kate Hudson was here

00:01:49.776 --> 00:01:52.178
yesterday talking about how
wonderful a group of people
this was.

00:01:52.178 --> 00:01:54.247
It seems like it was
an incredibly fun group

00:01:54.247 --> 00:01:56.582
of people to make a film with.
-Oh, absolutely.

00:01:56.582 --> 00:01:59.319
Like, first of all,
they are all icons.

00:01:59.319 --> 00:02:02.555
Kate Hudson. Come on.
Who kills it in this film.

00:02:02.555 --> 00:02:04.090
And Daniel.

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.259
It's just been a dream role,
a dream cast.

00:02:07.259 --> 00:02:10.696
And whenever we're all
getting celebrated together,

00:02:10.696 --> 00:02:12.198
that always makes me feel good.

00:02:12.198 --> 00:02:13.733
Because I got a real opportunity

00:02:13.733 --> 00:02:16.469
to, like, make some
life-long friendships.

00:02:16.469 --> 00:02:21.140
It felt like we were in a
theater summer camp repertoire,

00:02:21.140 --> 00:02:22.808
and you just wanted to call home
and be like,

00:02:22.808 --> 00:02:24.610
"Mom, don't come back
and get me.

00:02:24.610 --> 00:02:26.646
Leave me here
for another good month."

00:02:26.646 --> 00:02:28.047
-You did that as a kid, right?

00:02:28.047 --> 00:02:30.616
Didn't you do sort of --
A theater campy...

00:02:30.616 --> 00:02:33.252
-Yeah.
-And monologue camp.

00:02:33.252 --> 00:02:34.621
What was that?

00:02:34.621 --> 00:02:36.021
-Yes, I'm an
international thespian.

00:02:36.021 --> 00:02:37.724
-Yes, absolutely.
-Where are all of my thespians?

00:02:37.724 --> 00:02:39.058
-You are.

00:02:39.058 --> 00:02:41.361
-I was always in either
a Shakespearean program.

00:02:41.361 --> 00:02:44.063
I wrote for the Young
Playwrights' Roundtable --

00:02:44.063 --> 00:02:45.464
short stories.

00:02:45.464 --> 00:02:47.133
-At what age did you know

00:02:47.133 --> 00:02:50.136
that was the path you wanted
to take -- writing, performing?

00:02:50.136 --> 00:02:52.605
-Whew. Uh, last week.
-Okay.

00:02:52.605 --> 00:02:54.040
-Yeah. Last week.

00:02:54.040 --> 00:02:56.208
-Well, then, this must be
a great week of feedback, yeah.

00:02:56.208 --> 00:02:59.345
-No, I mean, my mom -- I had
the best parents, you know.

00:02:59.345 --> 00:03:00.679
They were working-class.

00:03:00.679 --> 00:03:02.648
And even though
they were working,

00:03:02.648 --> 00:03:04.317
they never missed
a talent showcase.

00:03:04.317 --> 00:03:05.885
There never was a time
in my life

00:03:05.885 --> 00:03:07.987
where I wasn't singing
or acting.

00:03:07.987 --> 00:03:10.390
Like, I was always in the music.

00:03:10.390 --> 00:03:12.825
All of it
just has felt beautiful.

00:03:12.825 --> 00:03:15.895
Storytelling -- I think
that's been the common thread.

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.329
I love telling stories.

00:03:17.329 --> 00:03:19.732
Through music, through fashion,
through whatever it is.

00:03:19.732 --> 00:03:23.869
-I've really enjoyed this sort
of act with you as a performer,

00:03:23.869 --> 00:03:25.304
not just musically,

00:03:25.304 --> 00:03:27.106
but also, you know,
in films and television.

00:03:27.106 --> 00:03:28.808
I knew you first as a singer,

00:03:28.808 --> 00:03:30.676
and you first collaborated
with Ed Norton,

00:03:30.676 --> 00:03:32.811
who's in the film,
when you were the musical guest

00:03:32.811 --> 00:03:34.413
for his "SNL" in 2013.

00:03:34.413 --> 00:03:37.283
-I did. Wow. My eyebrows.

00:03:37.283 --> 00:03:39.552
Why was I --
-Your eyebrows were very good.

00:03:39.552 --> 00:03:40.919
-I'm sorry.

00:03:40.919 --> 00:03:42.988
I feel like they are just
very strong and arched.

00:03:42.988 --> 00:03:44.757
But, yes, that was
the first time we met.

00:03:44.757 --> 00:03:46.358
He was hosting.
I was musical guest.

00:03:46.358 --> 00:03:47.660
So it's like a dream to --

00:03:47.660 --> 00:03:49.829
-It must've been crazy
to be collaborating again.

00:03:49.829 --> 00:03:52.298
This is, for those
who don't know, a sequel

00:03:52.298 --> 00:03:54.800
to the wonderful film
"Knives Out."

00:03:54.800 --> 00:03:56.168
Daniel Craig is back.

00:03:56.168 --> 00:03:58.470
A whole new cast of characters.
A murder mystery.

00:03:58.470 --> 00:04:01.741
You host -- True story.
You host murder-mystery parties?

00:04:01.741 --> 00:04:03.309
-Oh, yes.
-Always?

00:04:03.309 --> 00:04:04.977
-Mafia. Assassin.

00:04:04.977 --> 00:04:07.313
Who plays murder-mystery
games in here?

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.549
Come on.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:09.549 --> 00:04:12.819
Like, all-out.
Family, friends. We love it.

00:04:12.819 --> 00:04:15.755
-Now, it does not surprise me,
and I doubt it will surprise

00:04:15.755 --> 00:04:17.623
anyone else,
that you dress up a little bit

00:04:17.623 --> 00:04:19.391
when you host
a murder-mystery party.

00:04:19.391 --> 00:04:20.993
-What is this?
-This is a photo.

00:04:20.993 --> 00:04:22.995
[ Laughter ]

00:04:22.995 --> 00:04:25.965
-That is Sir Hawthorne James.

00:04:25.965 --> 00:04:27.733
Wow.
-Sir Hawthorne James?

00:04:27.733 --> 00:04:29.935
And he had people over
for a murder-mystery party?

00:04:29.935 --> 00:04:32.672
-Well, actually,
that took place in Serbia.

00:04:32.672 --> 00:04:34.373
-Okay.
-That was a murder mystery

00:04:34.373 --> 00:04:35.774
that Rian Johnson,

00:04:35.774 --> 00:04:38.544
our writer-director of
"Glass Onion," invited us to.

00:04:38.544 --> 00:04:40.880
We couldn't go out and party

00:04:40.880 --> 00:04:43.649
and be in the streets
because of COVID.

00:04:43.649 --> 00:04:45.317
Nobody wanted to be the actor

00:04:45.317 --> 00:04:48.153
that, like, shut down the whole
production because you were,

00:04:48.153 --> 00:04:51.556
you know, at a strip club
in Belgrade with no mask on.

00:04:51.556 --> 00:04:52.992
-By the way, you know,

00:04:52.992 --> 00:04:54.793
respect when you're in Belgrade,
you know.

00:04:54.793 --> 00:04:56.529
It's a bummer to go,
then you come back,

00:04:56.529 --> 00:04:57.897
and they're like,
"Did you go to the strip clubs?"

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:59.365
You're like, "I didn't go
to the strip clubs."

00:04:59.365 --> 00:05:02.635
-So what we would do is we all
stayed at this hotel together,

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:05.838
and they would rent out
the top bar part of the hotel,

00:05:05.838 --> 00:05:08.374
and we would have
murder-mystery parties.

00:05:08.374 --> 00:05:10.575
We'd get drunk.
You know, we'd have fun.

00:05:10.575 --> 00:05:11.711
-That really speaks to --

00:05:11.711 --> 00:05:13.479
I'm very impressed
that Rian Johnson,

00:05:13.479 --> 00:05:15.148
who continues to write these

00:05:15.148 --> 00:05:18.417
incredibly well-constructed
murder mysteries,

00:05:18.417 --> 00:05:19.751
and yet still has time

00:05:19.751 --> 00:05:22.121
to put a murder mystery party
together for his cast.

00:05:22.121 --> 00:05:23.856
-That has absolutely nothing
to do

00:05:23.856 --> 00:05:25.291
with the murder mystery
of the film.

00:05:25.291 --> 00:05:27.126
-I think it's very clear to me
that

00:05:27.126 --> 00:05:28.761
if he wasn't writing
these incredible things,

00:05:28.761 --> 00:05:30.062
he would definitely
be a murderer.

00:05:30.062 --> 00:05:32.732
So I think we should be
very happy

00:05:32.732 --> 00:05:34.133
that he has chosen the path.

00:05:34.133 --> 00:05:35.567
-No, listen, Rian is --

00:05:35.567 --> 00:05:37.670
I've wanted to work with Rian
since I saw "Looper."

00:05:37.670 --> 00:05:39.238
-"Looper" is a great movie."
-Oh, my God, right?

00:05:39.238 --> 00:05:41.440
And I went down this whole
rabbit hole, saw everything.

00:05:41.440 --> 00:05:44.977
I said, "I don't care if he
calls me to do a two-second

00:05:44.977 --> 00:05:49.114
lip gloss commercial,
I'm saying yes."

00:05:49.114 --> 00:05:51.050
-And I hope, and by the way,
I hope by bringing it up,

00:05:51.050 --> 00:05:52.652
people go back
and revisit "Looper."

00:05:52.652 --> 00:05:55.554
-I really do, they should.
-So you --

00:05:55.554 --> 00:05:57.656
It also won't surprise people,
Halloween.

00:05:57.656 --> 00:05:59.759
These are Halloweens.
Are these all Halloweens?

00:05:59.759 --> 00:06:01.160
-Yes, those are Halloweens.

00:06:01.160 --> 00:06:02.928
-I mean, so --
alright, first of all,

00:06:02.928 --> 00:06:05.764
my dad dressed up like
the white rabbit for my kids.

00:06:05.764 --> 00:06:07.232
-What?
Let me see that.

00:06:07.232 --> 00:06:09.135
-So he's the grandfather,
and I feel bad because I feel

00:06:09.135 --> 00:06:10.269
like he did a really good job,

00:06:10.269 --> 00:06:12.304
and then I'm going
to show yours.

00:06:12.304 --> 00:06:13.405
So there's my dad.
-Aww.

00:06:13.405 --> 00:06:15.508
-I mean, not bad, right?
-They're so cute!

00:06:15.508 --> 00:06:18.243
-He went out, he got a clock.

00:06:18.243 --> 00:06:20.245
But then,
there is Janelle Monáe.

00:06:20.245 --> 00:06:22.081
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:06:22.081 --> 00:06:24.182
It's deeply, deeply unfair.

00:06:24.182 --> 00:06:26.018
-That was cool.

00:06:26.018 --> 00:06:28.387
Yes, this was my white rabbit,
you know,

00:06:28.387 --> 00:06:30.156
from "Alice in Wonderland."

00:06:30.156 --> 00:06:31.224
I had so much fun.

00:06:31.224 --> 00:06:32.491
I'm telling you, living in L.A.,

00:06:32.491 --> 00:06:34.961
you just have some access
to these movie people.

00:06:34.961 --> 00:06:37.095
And I feel like my costumes
have gotten better.

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:39.732
It used to be Party City,
and now it's this.

00:06:39.732 --> 00:06:42.468
-I mean, like this, they were
like, "Oh, that's Grandpa."

00:06:42.468 --> 00:06:44.837
This, I think they would
just [bleep] their pants.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:47.607
[ Laughter ]

00:06:47.607 --> 00:06:50.510
I mean, you do really
take care of L.A.

00:06:50.510 --> 00:06:52.077
This is "Fifth Element."
Is this from --

00:06:52.077 --> 00:06:54.112
-Yep, Diva Plavalaguna.
-That's insane.

00:06:54.112 --> 00:06:57.416
And now is this a hard --
Do you regret this at all over

00:06:57.416 --> 00:06:59.785
the course of the night
sort of moving around?

00:06:59.785 --> 00:07:01.186
-[ Laughter ]
-You know what?

00:07:01.186 --> 00:07:03.622
Okay. That was five hours...
-Okay.

00:07:03.622 --> 00:07:05.691
-...of prosthetics sitting
in a chair.

00:07:05.691 --> 00:07:10.529
And the first -- The head that
they gave me, it was too heavy.

00:07:10.529 --> 00:07:12.831
So I had to have them
lift it up

00:07:12.831 --> 00:07:14.133
as I was getting my makeup on.

00:07:14.133 --> 00:07:17.536
And then I was like -- I
had to do a photo shoot in it.

00:07:17.536 --> 00:07:19.538
And then I did this again
for another four hours

00:07:19.538 --> 00:07:21.307
because I wanted
to attend parties.

00:07:21.307 --> 00:07:22.974
And I was like -- But I cannot

00:07:22.974 --> 00:07:24.876
have that big head
holding me down.

00:07:24.876 --> 00:07:27.480
And so they re-created it,
made it more light.

00:07:27.480 --> 00:07:29.281
And I went out
and I partied with it.

00:07:29.281 --> 00:07:30.716
And it was great.
-I'm very impressed.

00:07:30.716 --> 00:07:32.251
I mean, by the way,
that would be --

00:07:32.251 --> 00:07:33.419
If I saw you walking in,

00:07:33.419 --> 00:07:35.421
I've never had
a Halloween costume

00:07:35.421 --> 00:07:36.756
that's not a zero out of 10
compared to that.

00:07:36.756 --> 00:07:37.890
-I was like this the whole time.

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:40.726
[ Vocalizes ]

00:07:40.726 --> 00:07:44.330
Like, I literally would do that
at the Halloween party.

00:07:44.330 --> 00:07:45.231
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:45.231 --> 00:07:46.532
I don't know what's wrong
with me.

00:07:46.532 --> 00:07:48.501
-I saved this one for last
because it's Halloween.

00:07:48.501 --> 00:07:50.903
But it's very much
in the Christmas season.

00:07:50.903 --> 00:07:53.306
I mean, again,
mind-blowingly good.

00:07:53.306 --> 00:07:54.640
There you are as the Grinch.

00:07:54.640 --> 00:07:56.808
[ Cheers ]
-That's Johnny Grinch.

00:07:56.808 --> 00:07:58.077
-And then, are you eating?

00:07:58.077 --> 00:08:00.279 line:0%
What is that?
-What?

00:08:00.279 --> 00:08:02.648 line:0%
That's green eggs and ham.
-Okay.

00:08:02.648 --> 00:08:03.815 line:0%
-Yeah.
-[ Laughter ]

00:08:03.815 --> 00:08:06.719 line:0%
-I mean, that's a different
book, okay?

00:08:06.719 --> 00:08:08.020
-[ Laughter ]
-Yeah.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:09.821
-Like, I'm in the thick of it
right now,

00:08:09.821 --> 00:08:12.157
so don't go crossing
your Seusses on me.

00:08:12.157 --> 00:08:13.258
-I know, but it's Seuss.

00:08:13.258 --> 00:08:15.261
You know, it's Seuss.
Seuss food.

00:08:15.261 --> 00:08:18.897
-You have a Grinch idea.
-I do.

00:08:18.897 --> 00:08:20.299
Ooh, I do.

00:08:20.299 --> 00:08:24.270
So, first of all, those were
the lashes from Jim Carrey.

00:08:24.270 --> 00:08:25.771
-Really?
-As Grinch.

00:08:25.771 --> 00:08:27.773
Those were a replica
of those lashes.

00:08:27.773 --> 00:08:29.241
I just want that to be known.
-Okay, great.

00:08:29.241 --> 00:08:33.346
-Jim, you are the GOAT.
I do have an idea.

00:08:33.346 --> 00:08:37.349
I think if you go on my page,
you'll see the Johnny Grinch

00:08:37.349 --> 00:08:38.951
is like this sort of hipster.

00:08:38.951 --> 00:08:43.355
Like, you know, vegan and
green eggs and ham or wait.

00:08:43.355 --> 00:08:45.124
-Not a great vegan.
-No, not really a good vegan.

00:08:45.124 --> 00:08:47.026
-[ Laughter ]
-Anyway, forget that,

00:08:47.026 --> 00:08:48.460
forget that, forget that.
I'm off course.

00:08:48.460 --> 00:08:50.029
But the pitch is what

00:08:50.029 --> 00:08:52.999
if the Grinch had
an illegitimate child.

00:08:52.999 --> 00:08:54.232
-Okay.

00:08:54.232 --> 00:08:56.969
-Like he didn't know
that he had Johnny,

00:08:56.969 --> 00:09:00.439
and she somehow brings him back.
-Alright.

00:09:00.439 --> 00:09:02.741
And this is Grinch --
where does this Grinch,

00:09:02.741 --> 00:09:05.611
where does the illegitimate
Grinch land on Christmas?

00:09:05.611 --> 00:09:07.947
-I think that she doesn't
like Christmas.

00:09:07.947 --> 00:09:09.015
-Yeah.

00:09:09.015 --> 00:09:10.850
-And she starts
to uncover the why.

00:09:10.850 --> 00:09:14.887
And then maybe they both --
he likes Christmas now, right?

00:09:14.887 --> 00:09:15.988
-Obviously.

00:09:15.988 --> 00:09:18.290
His heart is three times bigger.
-Exactly.

00:09:18.290 --> 00:09:21.660
And then like somehow you go
a day in the life with her.

00:09:21.660 --> 00:09:24.697
Like she is in college,
maybe she is in med school.

00:09:24.697 --> 00:09:26.399
Maybe she is in a rock band.

00:09:26.399 --> 00:09:29.101
Like she lives
this really cool life.

00:09:29.101 --> 00:09:30.569
Like you want to hang out
with her.

00:09:30.569 --> 00:09:33.472
She drives an electric car,
and she has to go meet her dad,

00:09:33.472 --> 00:09:35.574
who is Jim Carrey, the Grinch.

00:09:35.574 --> 00:09:38.843
-This seems like a drama, but --
-You think it's a drama?

00:09:38.843 --> 00:09:41.413
Okay, yeah, you're right.

00:09:41.413 --> 00:09:43.716
But maybe they don't
like each other.

00:09:43.716 --> 00:09:46.619
Like she just does not like
her dad, because you left me.

00:09:46.619 --> 00:09:48.687
-Yeah.
I also want to hear this story

00:09:48.687 --> 00:09:50.389
about how he's like,
"I had no idea."

00:09:50.389 --> 00:09:52.424
I'm like, "Alright, Grinch.

00:09:52.424 --> 00:09:53.759
You lived alone in a cave.

00:09:53.759 --> 00:09:55.994
And you're like,
'I don't know how I'm a dad.'

00:09:55.994 --> 00:09:57.730
You know."
-Right.

00:09:57.730 --> 00:09:59.431
-I'll tell you where he went
when he was in Belgrade.

00:09:59.431 --> 00:10:02.234
He wasn't doing murder
mystery parties.

00:10:02.234 --> 00:10:03.402
Hey, thank you so much
for being here.

00:10:03.402 --> 00:10:04.670
It's always so great to see you.

00:10:04.670 --> 00:10:06.004
-Thank you, my friend.
I adore you.

00:10:06.004 --> 00:10:08.541
-Janelle Monáe, "Glass Onion:
A Knives Out Mystery"

00:10:08.541 --> 00:10:11.544
begins streaming on Netflix
December 23rd.

00:10:11.544 --> 00:10:13.245
We'll be right back
with Paul Dano.

